  "client_view_text": " The write-ahead log is a separate file where each write operation is recorded before it is applied to the main database ...\nWrite-ahead logging allows a database to recover from crashes or failures by recording all write operations in a separate file, called the write-ahead log, before they are applied to the main database. This ensures that the database remains in a consistent state even if the database crashes or fails. The database can recover its consistency by replaying all the write operations recorded in the write-ahead log, effectively undoing any partial writes that may have occurred before the crash. By doing so, the database returns to a consistent state, and all data is recoverable. In this way, write-ahead logging ensures data integrity and minimizes the risk of data loss in the event of a crash or failure. Additionally, write-ahead logging can be used to implement other database features, such as backup and recovery, and replication. Overall, write-ahead logging is a key component of many database systems, providing a reliable way to ensure data consistency and integrity in the face of system failures. ... (read more)\n1. The write-ahead log records all write operations before they are applied to the main database, allowing the database to recover its consistency after a crash or failure.\n2. The log contains a record"
